    {
      "id": 320,
      "type": "PixaromaNote",
      "pos": [
        -492.55818515076214,
        1352.4034476120892
      ],
      "size": [
        497.56924468078614,
        1024.5129195745849
      ],
      "flags": {},
      "order": 8,
      "mode": 0,
      "inputs": [],
      "outputs": [],
      "title": "About the render size",
      "properties": {
        "cnr_id": "comfyui-pixaroma",
        "ver": "2fe16a657e32aa6f7d4cfd0354cf184991545bac",
        "Node name for S&R": "PixaromaNote"
      },
      "widgets_values": [
        "{\"version\": 1, \"content\": \"<h1>RENDER SIZE</h1><p>\\n\\n</p><hr class=\\\"pix-note-hr-solid\\\" style=\\\"color: #b8b8b8\\\"><p>\\n\\n</p><p>Every size in the list was run on a 24 GB RTX 4090 and checked both ways round. Each one came out at the exact size asked for, with no stretching and nothing repeated, and kept the angle of your 3D view.</p><p>\\n\\n</p><table class=\\\"pix-note-grid\\\" style=\\\"--pix-note-grid-border: #f66744; --pix-note-grid-header-bg: #1a1a1a\\\"><thead><tr><th>Size</th><th>Shape</th><th>Time</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1024 x 1024</td><td>square</td><td>2.6 s</td></tr><tr><td>896 x 1120</td><td>4:5</td><td>2.5 s</td></tr><tr><td>864 x 1152</td><td>3:4</td><td>2.5 s</td></tr><tr><td>832 x 1248</td><td>2:3</td><td>2.7 s</td></tr><tr><td>720 x 1280</td><td>9:16</td><td>2.3 s</td></tr><tr><td>864 x 1536</td><td>9:16</td><td>3.3 s</td></tr><tr><td>1280 x 1280</td><td>square</td><td>4.2 s</td></tr><tr><td>1536 x 1536</td><td>square</td><td>6.9 s</td></tr></tbody></table><p>\\n\\n</p><p>Anything around one megapixel costs about the same, 2.3 to 2.7 seconds, so pick the shape you want rather than the smallest one. Going bigger is what costs time, because the 3D view is photographed at that size too and the model reads the bigger photograph as well. The first run after starting ComfyUI adds about 7 seconds while the model loads.</p><p>\\n\\n</p><p>Use <span style=\\\"color: #f66744\\\">Portrait</span> / <span style=\\\"color: #f66744\\\">Landscape</span> on the node to flip any of them, at the same speed. In Portrait, 4:5 is the Instagram portrait post and 9:16 is Reels, Stories, TikTok and Shorts. In Landscape, 9:16 turns into 16:9 for YouTube (1280 x 720 is exactly a YouTube thumbnail), and 2:3 and 3:4 turn into the usual 3:2 and 4:3 photo shapes.</p><p>\\n\\n</p><h2><span data-ic=\\\"checkmark\\\" class=\\\"pix-note-ic\\\" contenteditable=\\\"false\\\" style=\\\"color: #f66744\\\"></span>&nbsp;The 3D grid</h2><p>The grid in the 3D view is only there to help you line things up. It is left out of the photograph the model works from, so you can leave it on.</p><p>\\n\\n</p><h2><span data-ic=\\\"checkmark\\\" class=\\\"pix-note-ic\\\" contenteditable=\\\"false\\\" style=\\\"color: #f66744\\\"></span>&nbsp;Shape and angle</h2><p>The render size sets the size of the 3D photograph too. Its two outputs are also wired into <span style=\\\"color: #f66744\\\">Width and Height on the Load 3D node</span>, so those lock to what you pick here, and the frame in the 3D view shows the shape before you Run.</p><p>So the model always gets a photograph the same shape as the picture it has to fill, and it keeps the angle in every shape, tall ones included. Tested on a very tall 640 x 1536 frame with four seeds: <span style=\\\"color: #f66744\\\">all four kept the angle</span>. With those two wires unplugged the photograph goes back to the 1024 x 1024 typed on the Load 3D node, and all four seeds turned the weapon instead.</p><p>The picture copies the framing of the 3D view as well. Zoom in until the object fills the frame. If part of it is cut off, the close-up was kept on 4 of 5 seeds, but the model tends to invent lettering on big flat panels there.</p><p>\\n\\n</p>\", \"buttonColor\": \"#f66744\", \"lineColor\": \"#f66744\", \"width\": 480.0, \"height\": 1127.0, \"backgroundColor\": \"#2a2a2a\"}",
        ""
      ],
      "widgets_values_named": {
        "note_json": "{\"version\": 1, \"content\": \"<h1>RENDER SIZE</h1><p>\\n\\n</p><hr class=\\\"pix-note-hr-solid\\\" style=\\\"color: #b8b8b8\\\"><p>\\n\\n</p><p>Every size in the list was run on a 24 GB RTX 4090 and checked both ways round. Each one came out at the exact size asked for, with no stretching and nothing repeated, and kept the angle of your 3D view.</p><p>\\n\\n</p><table class=\\\"pix-note-grid\\\" style=\\\"--pix-note-grid-border: #f66744; --pix-note-grid-header-bg: #1a1a1a\\\"><thead><tr><th>Size</th><th>Shape</th><th>Time</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1024 x 1024</td><td>square</td><td>2.6 s</td></tr><tr><td>896 x 1120</td><td>4:5</td><td>2.5 s</td></tr><tr><td>864 x 1152</td><td>3:4</td><td>2.5 s</td></tr><tr><td>832 x 1248</td><td>2:3</td><td>2.7 s</td></tr><tr><td>720 x 1280</td><td>9:16</td><td>2.3 s</td></tr><tr><td>864 x 1536</td><td>9:16</td><td>3.3 s</td></tr><tr><td>1280 x 1280</td><td>square</td><td>4.2 s</td></tr><tr><td>1536 x 1536</td><td>square</td><td>6.9 s</td></tr></tbody></table><p>\\n\\n</p><p>Anything around one megapixel costs about the same, 2.3 to 2.7 seconds, so pick the shape you want rather than the smallest one. Going bigger is what costs time, because the 3D view is photographed at that size too and the model reads the bigger photograph as well. The first run after starting ComfyUI adds about 7 seconds while the model loads.</p><p>\\n\\n</p><p>Use <span style=\\\"color: #f66744\\\">Portrait</span> / <span style=\\\"color: #f66744\\\">Landscape</span> on the node to flip any of them, at the same speed. In Portrait, 4:5 is the Instagram portrait post and 9:16 is Reels, Stories, TikTok and Shorts. In Landscape, 9:16 turns into 16:9 for YouTube (1280 x 720 is exactly a YouTube thumbnail), and 2:3 and 3:4 turn into the usual 3:2 and 4:3 photo shapes.</p><p>\\n\\n</p><h2><span data-ic=\\\"checkmark\\\" class=\\\"pix-note-ic\\\" contenteditable=\\\"false\\\" style=\\\"color: #f66744\\\"></span>&nbsp;The 3D grid</h2><p>The grid in the 3D view is only there to help you line things up. It is left out of the photograph the model works from, so you can leave it on.</p><p>\\n\\n</p><h2><span data-ic=\\\"checkmark\\\" class=\\\"pix-note-ic\\\" contenteditable=\\\"false\\\" style=\\\"color: #f66744\\\"></span>&nbsp;Shape and angle</h2><p>The render size sets the size of the 3D photograph too. Its two outputs are also wired into <span style=\\\"color: #f66744\\\">Width and Height on the Load 3D node</span>, so those lock to what you pick here, and the frame in the 3D view shows the shape before you Run.</p><p>So the model always gets a photograph the same shape as the picture it has to fill, and it keeps the angle in every shape, tall ones included. Tested on a very tall 640 x 1536 frame with four seeds: <span style=\\\"color: #f66744\\\">all four kept the angle</span>. With those two wires unplugged the photograph goes back to the 1024 x 1024 typed on the Load 3D node, and all four seeds turned the weapon instead.</p><p>The picture copies the framing of the 3D view as well. Zoom in until the object fills the frame. If part of it is cut off, the close-up was kept on 4 of 5 seeds, but the model tends to invent lettering on big flat panels there.</p><p>\\n\\n</p>\", \"buttonColor\": \"#f66744\", \"lineColor\": \"#f66744\", \"width\": 480.0, \"height\": 1127.0, \"backgroundColor\": \"#2a2a2a\"}",
        "note_dom": ""
      },
      "color": "#1d1d1d",
      "bgcolor": "#2a2a2a"
    },
    {
      "id": 319,
      "type": "PixaromaNote",
      "pos": [
        1350.4695989660645,
        222.55671201638614
      ],
      "size": [
        751.1560964538571,
        635.3807173059417
      ],
      "flags": {},
      "order": 9,
      "mode": 0,
      "inputs": [],
      "outputs": [],
      "title": "Settings for your card",
      "properties": {
        "cnr_id": "ComfyUI-Pixaroma",
        "ver": "56e32d1676cab6700c54e4bdd37925f10867b970",
        "Node name for S&R": "PixaromaNote"
      },
      "widgets_values": [
        "{\"version\": 1, \"content\": \"<h1>SETTINGS FOR YOUR CARD</h1><p>\\n\\n</p><hr class=\\\"pix-note-hr-solid\\\" style=\\\"color: #b8b8b8\\\"><p>\\n\\n</p><p>This workflow only redraws a picture, so there is nothing to tune for quality except the render size, which has its own note next to that node.</p><p>\\n\\n</p><h2><span data-ic=\\\"chip\\\" class=\\\"pix-note-ic\\\" contenteditable=\\\"false\\\" style=\\\"color: #f66744\\\"></span>&nbsp;What it costs</h2><table class=\\\"pix-note-grid\\\" style=\\\"--pix-note-grid-border: #f66744; --pix-note-grid-header-bg: #1a1a1a\\\"><thead><tr><th></th><th>Measured on a 24 GB RTX 4090</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>A render at about 1 megapixel</td><td>2.6 seconds</td></tr><tr><td>The biggest size, 1536 x 1536</td><td>6.9 seconds</td></tr><tr><td>The first run after starting ComfyUI</td><td>about 7 seconds longer</td></tr><tr><td>Memory in use at the busiest moment</td><td>21.4 GB</td></tr></tbody></table><p>\\n\\n</p><p>That 21.4 GB is what ComfyUI chose to use when it had 24 GB free, not what the workflow needs. It spreads out when there is room and packs down when there is not, so a smaller card can still run this. It was only measured on the big card here, so treat a small card as untested rather than ruled out.</p><p>\\n\\n</p><h2><span data-ic=\\\"checkmark\\\" class=\\\"pix-note-ic\\\" contenteditable=\\\"false\\\" style=\\\"color: #f66744\\\"></span>&nbsp;The sampler</h2><p>Four steps at a CFG of 1 with <span style=\\\"color: #f66744\\\">euler</span> and <span style=\\\"color: #f66744\\\">simple</span>. Those are the turbo settings this model is built for and they do not need changing. If you want slightly crisper edges, try <span style=\\\"color: #f66744\\\">6 to 8 steps</span> - it costs roughly that much more time.</p><p>\\n\\n</p><h2><span data-ic=\\\"warning-v1\\\" class=\\\"pix-note-ic\\\" contenteditable=\\\"false\\\" style=\\\"color: #f66744\\\"></span>&nbsp;If you run out of memory</h2><ul><li><b>Lower the render size</b> first. It is by far the biggest lever here.</li><li>Close other programs using the graphics card, browsers included.</li><li>The 3D model itself barely matters. A 50 MB textured .glb loads no slower than a 6 MB one, because only the picture of it reaches the sampler.</li></ul><p>\\n\\n</p>\", \"buttonColor\": \"#f66744\", \"lineColor\": \"#f66744\", \"width\": 730, \"height\": 665, \"backgroundColor\": \"#2a2a2a\"}",
        ""
      ],
      "widgets_values_named": {
        "note_json": "{\"version\": 1, \"content\": \"<h1>SETTINGS FOR YOUR CARD</h1><p>\\n\\n</p><hr class=\\\"pix-note-hr-solid\\\" style=\\\"color: #b8b8b8\\\"><p>\\n\\n</p><p>This workflow only redraws a picture, so there is nothing to tune for quality except the render size, which has its own note next to that node.</p><p>\\n\\n</p><h2><span data-ic=\\\"chip\\\" class=\\\"pix-note-ic\\\" contenteditable=\\\"false\\\" style=\\\"color: #f66744\\\"></span>&nbsp;What it costs</h2><table class=\\\"pix-note-grid\\\" style=\\\"--pix-note-grid-border: #f66744; --pix-note-grid-header-bg: #1a1a1a\\\"><thead><tr><th></th><th>Measured on a 24 GB RTX 4090</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>A render at about 1 megapixel</td><td>2.6 seconds</td></tr><tr><td>The biggest size, 1536 x 1536</td><td>6.9 seconds</td></tr><tr><td>The first run after starting ComfyUI</td><td>about 7 seconds longer</td></tr><tr><td>Memory in use at the busiest moment</td><td>21.4 GB</td></tr></tbody></table><p>\\n\\n</p><p>That 21.4 GB is what ComfyUI chose to use when it had 24 GB free, not what the workflow needs. It spreads out when there is room and packs down when there is not, so a smaller card can still run this. It was only measured on the big card here, so treat a small card as untested rather than ruled out.</p><p>\\n\\n</p><h2><span data-ic=\\\"checkmark\\\" class=\\\"pix-note-ic\\\" contenteditable=\\\"false\\\" style=\\\"color: #f66744\\\"></span>&nbsp;The sampler</h2><p>Four steps at a CFG of 1 with <span style=\\\"color: #f66744\\\">euler</span> and <span style=\\\"color: #f66744\\\">simple</span>. Those are the turbo settings this model is built for and they do not need changing. If you want slightly crisper edges, try <span style=\\\"color: #f66744\\\">6 to 8 steps</span> - it costs roughly that much more time.</p><p>\\n\\n</p><h2><span data-ic=\\\"warning-v1\\\" class=\\\"pix-note-ic\\\" contenteditable=\\\"false\\\" style=\\\"color: #f66744\\\"></span>&nbsp;If you run out of memory</h2><ul><li><b>Lower the render size</b> first. It is by far the biggest lever here.</li><li>Close other programs using the graphics card, browsers included.</li><li>The 3D model itself barely matters. A 50 MB textured .glb loads no slower than a 6 MB one, because only the picture of it reaches the sampler.</li></ul><p>\\n\\n</p>\", \"buttonColor\": \"#f66744\", \"lineColor\": \"#f66744\", \"width\": 730, \"height\": 665, \"backgroundColor\": \"#2a2a2a\"}",
        "note_dom": ""
      },
      "color": "#1d1d1d",
      "bgcolor": "#2a2a2a"
    }
